academictransfer:363294.description:
Are you fascinated by marine microbes? Are you interested in the dynamics of microalgal communities, and how they are affected by environmental change? Do you like to combine laboratory experiments with molecular methods and fieldwork in polar ecosystems? We have a vacancy for a postdoctoral researcher within the NWO-funded project ‘The key role of mixotrophy in Antarctic Carbon and Sulfur Cycles’ (MACS).
 Join us! 
The Antarctic seasonal sea-ice zone is rapidly changing, with consequences for the key primary producers: microalgae growing in sea-ice and water. While traditionally regarded as purely photoautotrophic, many of these microalgae likely also feed on other microbes or take up dissolved organic matter. Such a mixotrophic lifestyle might help them to survive the polar night and allow them an early start in the growing season, long before other algae receive sufficient light to pick up growth. Additionally, microalgae contribute to the production of the trace gas dimethylsulfide (DMS) that has an impact on climate via cloud formation. The MACS project aims to understand the role of mixotrophic protists in Antarctic C- and S-cycles. Such mechanistic understanding will help predict how changing ice cover will affect microalgal growth and DMS-production in the future, and whether feedbacks might be expected in a changing climate.
 What are you going to do? 
As a postdoc in the MACS project you will investigate how multiple environmental stressors affect the community composition, physiology, and gene expression of Antarctic microalgae, with a particular focus on their mixotrophic nutrition. You will resolve how mixotrophy contributes to carbon cycling during ice melt in spring and how this contribution depends on environmental conditions. You will perform laboratory experiments and fieldwork in collaboration with two other postdocs within the project.
 Tasks and responsibilities: 
 Design and perform laboratory experiments with cultured microalgae to assess their response to multiple environmental factors under controlled conditions;
 Participate in a research expedition to Rothera station, West Antarctic Peninsula, to assess microalgal species composition and perform field experiments to quantify their rates of feeding and photosynthesis;
 Publication of the results in international scientific journals;
 Presentation of the results in the department, at meetings of the Netherlands Polar Program, and at (international) scientific conferences;
 Supervision of BSc and/or MSc students who wish to do an internship on Antarctic microalgae.
 Your profile 
We are looking for an enthusiastic candidate, who enjoys a combination of laboratory experiments and field studies. You have a keen interest in marine microalgal ecology and are eager to perform research in Antarctica. You are a well-organized team player, but also able to work independently, and enjoy collaboration in an international team.
 Your experience and profile: 
 A PhD degree in phytoplankton ecology, environmental microbiology, biological oceanography or a related field;
 Experience with culturing aquatic microorganisms, physiological assays and fieldwork in marine ecosystems;
 Experience with omics-techniques and bioinformatic/computational analyses are a plus;
 Willingness and ability to perform extended fieldwork at Rothera station, Antarctica;
 Strong communication skills in English.
 Our offer 
We offer a temporary employment contract for preferably 38 hours per week for a period of 1 year, with the possibility to the extend the contract for another 15 months upon a positive evaluation of your performance. The preferred starting date is 1 January 2027. For this position the University Job Classification profile Researcher 4 applies.
The gross monthly salary, based on 38 hours per week and dependent on relevant experience, ranges between € 3,706 to € 5,760 (scale 10). In addition, you will receive an 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% year-end allowance.
A favourable tax agreement, the ‘30% ruling’, may apply to non-Dutch applicants.
The Collective Labour Agreement of Universities of the Netherlands is applicable.
Besides the salary and a vibrant and challenging environment at Science Park we offer you multiple fringe benefits:
 232 holiday hours per year (based on fulltime) and extra holidays between Christmas and 1 January;
 multiple courses to follow from our Teaching and Learning Centre;
 a complete educational program for PhD students;
 multiple courses on topics such as leadership for academic staff;
 multiple courses on topics such as time management, handling stress and an online learning platform with 100+ different courses;
 7 weeks birth leave (partner leave) with 100% salary;
 partly paid parental leave;
 the possibility to set up a workplace at home;
 a pension at ABP for which UvA pays two third part of the contribution;
 the possibility to follow courses to learn Dutch;
 help with registration at UvA- Housing for a studio or small ap

academictransfer:363320.description:
Als Medewerker Technische Dienst heb je een gevarieerde en uitdagende functie waar je ervoor zorgt dat onze duurzame gebouwen en installaties goed functioneren. Door jouw inzet blijft de techniek betrouwbaar – een onmisbare schakel in ons werk voor natuur en klimaat.
De Technische Dienst zorgt ervoor dat de installaties en apparatuur die nodig is voor het onderzoek in goede staat verkeren. Je kunt dan denken aan klimaat- en koelinstallaties, perslucht en speciaal gassen en kweekkasten. Ook beheert de Technische Dienst de gebouwinstallaties zoals de inbraakbeveiliging, het technisch netwerk en de verlichting. Regelmatig denk je samen met onderzoekers mee over praktische technische oplossingen van hun onderzoeksopstellingen en ondersteun je hiermee hun onderzoek.
Je voert het preventieve onderhoud uit, verhelpt storingen, en je houdt de planning en de documentatie bij. Je hebt regelmatig contact met aannemers en installateurs en je begeleidt hun werkzaamheden. Je stemt werkzaamheden af met de eigenaar van het gebouw (KNAW) en bedrijven die in hun opdracht werken.
Kleine onderhoudsprojecten voer je zelf uit of met hulp van je collega’s. Bij grote projecten adviseer je het projectteam en begeleid je de aannemer of installateur. Je neemt deel aan technisch overleg met de installateur. We verwachten dat je eens per vier weken meedraait in de storingsdienst.

REQUIREMENTS:
Plannen, prioriteren en organiseren van je werk is voor jou vanzelfsprekend en je bent gewend om zelfstandig aan een opdracht te werken. Tegelijkertijd schakel je gemakkelijk met je collega’s wanneer dat nodig is. Kwaliteit staat bij ons hoog in het vaandel. Met jouw flexibele instelling speel je in op de afwisselende werkzaamheden en je communiceert gemakkelijk met collega’s en externe partijen.
Je hebt:
 Een afgeronde MBO-opleiding in een technische richting;
 Kennis en ervaring met onderhoud en beheer van gebouwinstallaties;
 Kennis en ervaring met metaalbewerking (draaien, frezen, lassen etc.);
 Ervaring met andere technische vaardigheden zoals bijvoorbeeld 3D printen, CAD of elektrotechniek / elektronica is een pré;
 Je spreekt en schrijft goed Nederlands en Engels;
 Een BHV-certificaat of de bereidheid deze te behalen;
 Je bent woonachtig binnen 30 minuten reistijd van Wageningen (in verband met de storingsdienst);
 Je kunt creatieve, praktische oplossingen aandragen;
 Je staat stevig in je (werk)schoenen om de dagelijkse uitdagingen aan te gaan;
 Je bent een echte teamspeler die graag met collega’s binnen en buiten de afdeling samenwerkt om de technische uitdagingen aan te pakken.
Mocht je niet aan alle eisen voldoen, maar wel enthousiast zijn over de functie, dan nodigen we je van harte uit om te reageren. We staan open voor verschillende achtergronden en manieren waarop je kennis en ervaring hebt opgedaan.

CONTRACT: 1 jaar

academictransfer:363331.description:
Bij ons vind je een veelzijdige rol en werk je in een professioneel en bevlogen HR-team. Als Adviseur Arbeidsvoorwaarden combineer je een rol waarin je expertise in regelgeving en het ondersteunen van leidinggevenden en medewerkers op gebied van HR combineert.
Geen dag is hetzelfde: je schakelt moeiteloos tussen uiteenlopende vraagstukken, past de CAO-NU of andere regelgeving correct toe en je draagt bij aan het efficiënter maken van processen.
 Wat ga je doen? 
Je werkt samen met collega's van bedrijfsvoering, leidinggevenden en medewerkers binnen verschillende organisatieonderdelen. Voor hen ben jij een betrouwbare gesprekspartner, die meedenkt binnen de regelgevende kaders en passend advies geeft.
Naast deze operationele taken werk je actief mee aan procesverbetering en beleidsontwikkeling in kenniskringen of projectgroepen. Zo pas je je expertise toe op zowel uitvoerend als proces niveau.
 Je bent het eerste aanspreekpunt voor in, door- en uitstroom van medewerkers en adviseert bedrijfsvoering over HR-zaken en procedures;
 Je werkt nauw samen met de HR-Adviseur en samen vorm je een team voor de departementen en ketens van de faculteit;
 Je werkt samen met collega's van de bedrijfsvoering binnen de verschillende departementen en ketens, de personeels- en salarisadministratie en de International Service Desk. Samen zorg je voor een soepele uitvoering van de administratieve processen en een optimale dienstverlening;
 Je coördineert, controleert en bewaakt de correcte afhandeling van alle HR in- door – en uitstroom van de faculteit in het HR- systeem;
 Je verzorgt maandelijks een (online) presentatie over arbeidsvoorwaarden voor nieuwe medewerkers;
 Je bent in staat om arbeidsvoorwaarden en rechtspositie toe te passen en te vertalen naar werkprocessen en/of deze aan te passen;
 Je neemt deel aan team overleggen en universiteit brede kenniskringen, waardoor je je vermogen om onderbouwd te signaleren en adviseren verder versterkt;
 Je adviseert leidinggevenden over recruitmentvraagstukken en je verzorgt de verdere afhandeling van het gehele werving & selectieproces in het systeem;
 Je maakt overzichten in SAP en je onderhoudt samen met je collega’s SAP-OM, onze bedrijfsapplicatie;
 Je neemt deel aan projectgroepen voor het nieuwe SAP-systeem (Asturias) en draagt bij aan de toekomstbestendigheid van HR-processen.
Als Adviseur Arbeidsvoorwaarden werk je in een professioneel en bevlogen team van 13 HR-collega’s, waarvan 2 collega Adviseurs Arbeidsvoorwaarden. Betrokkenheid staat bij ons centraal: we werken in een open en positieve sfeer, helpen elkaar om het beste uit onszelf te halen en krijgen volop ruimte om ons verder te ontwikkelen.
In de dagelijkse praktijk werk je nauw samen met de HR-Adviseur die zich binnen het departement en/of keten richt op de strategische en tactische vraagstukken. Samen vormen jullie een sterk HR-duo voor het departement/keten.

REQUIREMENTS:
Je schakelt gemakkelijk tussen verschillende HR-vraagstukken en vertaalt complexe regelgeving naar duidelijke en bruikbare adviezen. Je plant je werkzaamheden zorgvuldig, houdt overzicht wanneer prioriteiten veranderen en werkt nauwkeurig met vertrouwelijke informatie. Je zoekt actief de samenwerking op met collega's en leidinggevenden en communiceert helder, zowel mondeling als schriftelijk. Daarnaast maak je nieuwe systemen snel eigen en lever je graag een bijdrage aan het verbeteren van processen.
Daarnaast beschik je over:
 een afgeronde hbo-opleiding, bij voorkeur in HRM of een vergelijkbare richting;
 ervaring met complexe HR-regelgeving en het toepassen van de cao Nederlandse Universiteiten of een vergelijkbare rechtspositie;
 goede beheersing van de Nederlandse en Engelse taal;
 ervaring met of aantoonbare affiniteit met HR-systemen; ervaring met SAP is een pluspunt.

academictransfer:363409.description:
We are seeking a motivated and versatile researcher to contribute to our work on water quality monitoring in marine ecosystems in both the Netherlands and the Dutch Caribbean. This position focuses on the integration of water quality monitoring, ecological indicators, and policy-relevant environmental assessments.
The successful candidate will play an important role in strengthening ongoing collaborations with local nature organizations, and policy stakeholders. The position combines fieldwork, data analysis, scientific assessment, and stakeholder engagement, offering a unique opportunity to contribute to sustainable marine management.
 As a researcher, your tasks and responsibilities will include: 
 prepare, conduct and process water quality measurements (including work on board of small research vessels);
 conduct advanced data management, statistical analysis and modelling of ecological and environmental stressor data using R, including OSPAR’s Harmonised Regional Seas Assessment Tool (HARSAT), to assess contaminant levels, biological effects, spatial and temporal trends, and environmental status. Experience with GIS and an affinity for additional programming languages, analytical tools and data-visualisation software are considered an advantage;
 report and present research findings;
 knowledge of eutrophication and water-quality processes in marine ecosystems, including their key drivers, ecological effects and interactions with chemical stressors, is also desirable;
 maintain and strengthen relationships with stakeholders and contribute to clear communication of scientific findings.
The Environmental Chemistry and Tropics teams aim to strengthen scientific knowledge and provide advice on contaminants in environmental matrices, such as water and sediment, as well as on internal contaminant concentrations in aquatic organisms. We seek to expand this expertise to tropical ecosystems, where ecological indicators must be integrated with measurements of chemical stress. A key focus of the position is therefore understanding and combining chemical, ecological and environmental data, including the factors that influence contaminant exposure, bioavailability, accumulation and ecological effects. By combining field measurements, laboratory analyses, data interpretation, and assessment methodologies, the team provides scientific support for environmental policy, ecosystem management, and the protection of aquatic ecosystems in the Netherlands.
 Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) in IJmuiden conducts applied scientific research to support the sustainable management of marine and coastal ecosystems. Our research provides knowledge and advice for governments, industry, and society on topics such as fisheries, biodiversity, water quality, ecosystem functioning, and climate change. Working closely with national and international partners, WMR combines field observations, monitoring programmes, data analysis, and innovative research to address environmental challenges in marine environments.

REQUIREMENTS:
We are looking for a candidate with:
 a completed MSc degree in Environmental Sciences, Earth Sciences, Marine Sciences, Water Managementor a related field;
 experience with one or more water-quality monitoring techniques, such as CTD profiling, nutrient and phytoplankton sampling, or comparable field and laboratory methods. Knowledge of marine biota, including their biology, ecology and responses to environmental stressors, is also desirable;
 willingness to travel regularly to conduct field sampling and monitoring activities in the North Sea and the Caribbean Netherlands;
 strong scientific writing and communication skills, with the ability to interpret, report and present complex chemical, ecological and statistical results clearly to both scientific and non-technical audiences;
 experience with data analysis and interpretation, including the use of R, GIS, Python, or comparable analytical tools;
 strong communication skills, with excellent written and spoken Dutch and English;
 knowledge of Papiamento and/or Spanish is highly desirable and considered a strong asset;
 the ability to work independently while contributing effectively within a multidisciplinary team.
In our international working environment, communication is increasingly conducted in English. For this position, a B1 level of English proficiency is required. If you have not yet reached this level, we will of course support you, and you can make use of the courses offered by our language centre, Wageningen in'to Languages .
As you can see, there are plenty of challenges awaiting you. These challenges can also be viewed as opportunities for professional development. Therefore, even if you do not yet have the exact track record requested in the vacancy, but do have solid relevant work experience, we would still very much like to encourage you to apply.

CONTRACT: 1 year

academictransfer:363449.description:
Als HR-adviseur ben jij de sparringpartner en procesbegeleider voor leidinggevenden en medewerkers bij uiteenlopende HR-vraagstukken. Je adviseert over de volle breedte van het HR-vak: van werving en selectie, vitaliteit en duurzame inzetbaarheid tot verzuim en uitstroom. Je houdt overzicht over de HR-processen, signaleert kansen voor verbetering en vertaalt deze naar concrete acties. Daarnaast lever je een actieve bijdrage aan organisatie brede HR-projecten.
Jouw werkzaamheden omvatten onder andere:
 Coördineren van werving en selectie waarbij je ook regelmatig sollicitatie- en arbeidsvoorwaardengesprekken voert;
 Informeren en adviseren van leidinggevenden en medewerkers over regelingen, rechtspositie en arbeidsvoorwaarden en de toepassing daarvan;
 Bewaken en ondersteunen van acties in het kader van Wet Verbetering Poortwachter en begeleiden van leidinggevenden bij ziekteverzuim binnen hun team;
 Aanvragen van verblijfsvergunningen en de 30% regeling voor internationale collega’s;
 Administreren van het instroom-, doorstroom- en uitstroomproces. Wij vinden het belangrijk dat dat juist en volledig gebeurt en dat wij voldoen aan interne en externe regelgeving.
Wij maken gebruik van elkaars talenten en vaardigheden. Je draagt bij aan het verder ontwikkelen van een specifiek HR gerelateerd onderwerp waar jij veel ervaring in hebt of je in wilt ontwikkelen. Kortom, een zeer diverse functie waarin je verantwoordelijk bent voor het hele HR-proces!

REQUIREMENTS:
Je bent een HR-professional die energie krijgt van een brede HR-rol waarin geen dag hetzelfde is. Je combineert een scherp oog voor detail met een mensgerichte aanpak en weet moeiteloos te schakelen tussen administratieve processen, advies en dagelijkse vragen van medewerkers en leidinggevenden. Je vindt het interessant om te werken met zelfstandige professionals. Je weet vertrouwen op te bouwen, maar bent ook in staat om vanuit jouw HR-expertise kaders te stellen en waar nodig een ander perspectief te bieden.
 Verder heb je:
 Minimaal 2 jaar relevante ervaring als HR-Adviseur of in een vergelijkbare brede rol;
 HBO werk- en denkniveau, bij voorkeur een afgeronde opleiding richting HRM;
 Je kent de wet- en regelgeving op het gebied van HR;
 Goede adviesvaardigheden, een proactieve houding, goed overzicht en overtuigingskracht;
 Je signaleert kansen, denkt mee en neemt initiatief om HR-processen verder te verbeteren.
 Je spreekt en schrijft goed Nederlands en Engels om zo goed te kunnen communiceren met jouw internationale collega’s.

CONTRACT: 12 maanden (met uitzicht op een vast contract)

academictransfer:363460.description:
Are you fascinated by how protein complexes allow plant cells to rapidly integrate environmental information? Do you have a passion for molecular biology, protein biochemistry and quantitative bio-imaging? Then this PhD position may be for you. We are looking for a dedicated PhD candidate to investigate the biochemical basis of rapid signal integration in plants.
 Join us in unlocking the secrets of plant signal integration! 
Plants must cope with a wide diversity of stresses such as drought, soil salinity and pathogen attack. Their survival hinges on the continuously perception, integration and interpretation of an immense number of signals from the environment and generating the appropriate responses. Millions of years of evolution have allowed plants to adapt to almost every climate and has made them to masters of perceiving and responding to countless signals. These signals usually occur simultaneously, yet plant cells must rapidly turn this complex input into coordinated responses. Although much is known about responses to individual signals, the molecular mechanisms by which plants use dedicated proteins to integrate multiple co-occurring signals remain largely unexplored.
We are seeking a motivated PhD candidate for the second PhD position in an NWO VIDI-funded project led by Dr. André Kuhn. This position focuses on elucidating the biochemical and cell-biological basis of a signal integration via B4-RAF kinase signalling complexes. You will join an ambitious, collaborative and international team in the Plant Cell Biology group within the Green Life Sciences cluster at the Swammerdam Institute for Life Sciences from the University of Amsterdam.
 This is what you will do 
The overarching project investigates how plant cells rapidly integrate co-occurring osmotic, salinity and pathogen-derived signals. Your PhD project will examine B4-RAF protein kinases as intracellular signal integrators. These kinases are activated rapidly by diverse environmental stimuli and occur in distinct subcellular puncta, suggesting that they assemble into signalling complexes (signalosomes) that coordinate early cellular responses.
You will (1) identify and validate the components of B4-RAF signalling complexes in Arabidopsis thaliana , (2) establish how these complexes assemble and localize under distinct environmental conditions, and (3) determine how these complexes contribute to stress resilience. The work combines plant molecular biology, protein biochemistry, functional proteomics, quantitative live-cell imaging and genetics.
Core research activities include:
 identify candidate B4-RAF signalosome components through immunoprecipitation coupled to tandem mass spectrometry (IP–MS/MS), building on existing proximity-labelling datasets;
 validate protein interactions and co-localisation using approaches such as FRET-FLIM, co-immunoprecipitation and live-cell confocal imaging;
 generate and characterise Arabidopsis loss-of-function and fluorescent reporter lines;
 use microfluidics-coupled live-cell imaging to investigate the spatiotemporal dynamics, environmental conditionality and subcellular localisation of B4-RAF signalling complexes;
 integrate proteomics, imaging and genetic datasets to develop mechanistic models of rapid plant signal integration.
 Tasks and responsibilities: 
 complete a PhD thesis within the official appointment duration (four years);
 systematically and independently design, perform, analyse and document experiments;
 be an active and responsible member of the research group
 collaborate closely with other group members (incl. PhD candidate, technicians and postdoc);
 discuss work with group members and at departmental meetings, and incorporate feedback;
 take a leading role in writing manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals;
 participate in the PhD training programme of the University of Amsterdam and the national graduate school Experimental Plant Sciences (EPS);
 assist with teaching and supervision of Bachelor’s and Master’s students.
You will have the opportunity to:
 work at the interface of plant genetics, biochemistry, functional proteomics and quantitative imaging;
 present results at national and international scientific conferences;
 expand academic, professional and personal skills;
 contribute to science communication and outreach activities;
 thrive in a team that values impactful research and mutual support;
 combine the advantages that a collaborative project offers, including access to state-of-the-art research facilities at the Swammerdam Institute and the University of Amsterdam.
 Your Profile 
You are passionate about science and have a particular interest in biochemistry and cell biology. You are eager to understanding complex environmental signalling mechanisms in plants at the molecular level and have a strong interest in protein signalling mechanisms, especially kinase regulation, protein complexes or phosphorylation-dependent signalling. You enjoy combining ex

academictransfer:363468.description:
Make an impact to sustainable soil and plant health.
Are you an ambitious scientist with expertise in soil ecology and plant-soil interactions? Do you enjoy acquiring and leading research projects and collaborating with partners across science, industry, farmers, and government? Then this position could be the perfect next step in your career.
As a Soil Ecology scientist at Wageningen Plant Research in Wageningen, you will contribute to understanding the role of soil organisms and translate these findings into practical management in diverse ecosystems, including agriculture, grasslands and more urban systems. You will play a key role in initiating, coordinating, and conducting (fundamental up to more applied) research projects together with multidisciplinary teams and external partners.
In this position, you will have a high degree of independence and responsibility. You will cooperate in running projects, identify new research opportunities and initiate/acquire new projects, lead and manage research projects, contribute to knowledge development and sharing, and work closely with colleagues, clients, and stakeholders from both the public and private sectors. We are looking for an enthusiastic and creative scientist with experience in the fields of soil ecology and plant health.
You will be based in Wageningen and collaborate with experts from related fields such as (meta)genomics, biocontrol, plant breeding, microbiome, phytopathology, bacteriology, nematology, entomology and diagnostics.
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 acquiring, coordinating, leading, and conducting research projects on soil ecology within multidisciplinary teams;
 reporting research outcomes through reports, scientific publications, trade articles, presentations, newsletters, and online communication channels;
 supervising project staff, interns, and students;
 building and maintaining strong relationships with clients and translating their questions into relevant and feasible research projects;
 translating research findings into practical recommendations and applications for clients and other stakeholders.
 Your team 
You will join Wageningen Plant Research, part of Wageningen University & Research. You will work together with colleagues who are dedicated to understand the role of soil ecology in diverse ecosystems and to developing innovative and sustainable solutions for diseases and pests in agriculture and horticulture.
While current crop protection agents reduce yield losses, they often may have a negative impact on people and the environment. Wageningen Plant Research develops solutions such as biological crop protection and resilient cropping systems, offering opportunities for sustainable food production worldwide. The interaction between plants, soils and diseases & pests is the focal point of our research.
We study the interactions between plants and harmful or beneficial organisms, including insects, fungi, viruses, and bacteria, both above and below ground. In addition, we develop advanced diagnostic methods to detect harmful organisms throughout the production chain.
The team is based in Wageningen and works closely with national and international partners from science, industry, and government.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are an ambitious, enthusiastic and proactive researcher with a background in soil ecology and a strong interest in sustainable crop protection and plant health. You enjoy working in multidisciplinary teams, coordinating projects, and building collaborations with clients and research partners. You are proactive, well-organised, and able to manage multiple projects simultaneously.
You are also capable of translating scientific knowledge into practical applications and communicating research results to both scientific and non-scientific audiences. In addition, you enjoy identifying new research opportunities and developing and acquiring project proposals for national and international funding programs or clients.
You communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing. Knowledge of the Dutch language is highly desirable. If you do not already speak Dutch, you are willing to learn it.
 You also possess: 
 a university degree in soil ecology, plant-soil interactions or a related field; a PhD is considered an advantage;
 experience with and/or affinity for: 
 Project acquisition;
 Plant health and crop protection;
 Microbiology;
 Nematology;
 Knowledge valorisation;
 Molecular diagnostics;
 Statistics;
 outreach;
 project management skills;
 excellent communication and networking skills;
 a collaborative and client-oriented mindset.
In our international working environment there is an increasing amount of communication in English. For this position it is about language level C1.

CONTRACT: 1 year

academictransfer:363469.description:
Are you interested in making bioeconomy innovations more sustainable? More specifically, evaluating biodiversity indicators on a local level, and engaging with stakeholders to assess impacts on climate change, welfare, and foresight future developments? Want to work in a global interdisciplinary team with members from 15 organizations, all within a EU-Horizon Project (CASI-BIO, “Cascading and Sustainable Innovation for the Bioeconomy”)? The work would entail a combination of qualitative and quantitative approaches, policy analysis, futures study, and participatory research within a context of a living lab in The Netherlands. 
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 co-coordinate a Work Package in a EU Horizon project;
 run workshops and focus groups with stakeholders in the Netherlands;
 develop tools to help analyze the impacts of Bioeconomy projects on environmental and socioeconomic outcomes;
 write and publish scientific papers and project reports.
 Your team 
You will work primarily with Dr. Jorge Sellare and Dr. Marko Lovric at WUR and will collaborate with other colleagues from the CASI-BIO project.
 You will work here 
You will work as part of the Forest and Nature Conservation Policy (FNP) Chair Group at Wageningen University. We are an interdisciplinary and international chair group composed of over 40 researchers who share an interest in various aspects of forests and nature.

REQUIREMENTS:
You have proven capacity to work both independently and as part of a team, and very good interpersonal communication skills to work together with non-academic stakeholders.
 You also possess: 
 a PhD in Business and Innovation Management, Economics, Environmental Sciences, Political Sciences, or related fields;
 work experience with stakeholder engagement;
 familiarity with mixed methods for scientific research;
 at least C1 level of Dutch.
In our international working environment there is an increasing amount of communication in English. For this position we require English language level C1 at least.

