academictransfer:363504.description:
Als onderzoeker kwelderecologie werk je aan het kwelderonderzoek van Wageningen Marine Research (WMR). Samen met het kwelderteam ga jij metingen en analyses doen naar de langjarige vegetatieontwikkeling en opslibbing, de effecten van bodemdaling door gaswinning en de koolstofopslag op kwelders. Deze kennis verwerk je in rapporten en je geeft advies over kwelderherstelplannen en beheer. Je draagt daarmee ook bij aan de ontwikkeling van "nature-based solutions" in kustgebieden en hoe deze op een ecologisch verantwoorde manier kunnen worden toegepast.
Je werkt samen met jouw collega’s aan meerdere projecten tegelijkertijd en de verantwoordelijkheden zijn verdeeld op basis van capaciteit en persoonlijke interesses. Contacten met klanten en andere belanghebbenden zijn een integraal onderdeel van ons werk.
 Jouw taken en verantwoordelijkheden als onderzoeker kwelderecologie zijn: 
 jaarlijkse monitoring van opslibbing en vegetatieontwikkeling op verschillende kwelders in de Waddenzee;
 analyse en interpretatie van monitoringsdata;
 het schrijven van rapporten aan klanten;
 assisteren met de verwerving van nieuwe projecten en het schrijven van onderzoeksvoorstellen;
 publiceren en presenteren van eigen onderzoek in wetenschappelijke tijdschriften en op conferenties.
 Wageningen Marine Research 
Jouw werkgebied is met name de Nederlandse Waddenzee, maar ook andere delen van Nederland en internationale projecten behoren tot de mogelijkheden. Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) is leidend in Nederland waar het gaat om ecologische dataverzameling op zee: bodemdieren, zeezoogdieren, vissen, vogels, koraal, schelpdieren, etc.. Veel van deze datasets worden al decennia lang gestandaardiseerd verzameld en onderhouden. Jij draagt bij aan de continuering van de kwelderdataset met meer dan 60 jaar aan gegevens.

REQUIREMENTS:
Jij bent een ondernemende onderzoeker met kennis van de ecologie van kwelders, schorren en zilte vegetaties en hebt een brede interesse in het Wadden- en kustecosysteem. Je kan omgaan met tijdsdruk en je kan werk rond deadlines plannen. Je bent gedreven, initiatiefrijk, gericht op samenwerken, klantgericht en communicatief sterk.
 Ook beschik jij over: 
 een afgeronde relevante academische opleiding in de Natuur- of Levenswetenschappen en een specialisatie in Wadden en kustecosystemen. Een promotie op het voor deze functie beschreven vakgebied is een pré;
 kennis van kweldervegetatie;
 ervaring met “nature based solutions”;
 een vlotte pen in het schrijven van rapportages;
 ervaring met statistische analyses om langjarige en ruimtelijke data te analyseren;
 rijbewijs B;
 beheersing van de Nederlandse taal is een pré.
In onze internationale werkomgeving wordt steeds meer in het Engels gecommuniceerd. Voor deze functie gaat het om taalniveau C1. Heb je dit niveau nog niet, dan helpen wij je vanzelfsprekend en kun je terecht bij ons taalcentrum Wageningen in’to Language .
Je ziet dat er voldoende uitdagingen voor jou liggen. Deze uitdagingen kunnen ook als ontwikkelmogelijkheden gezien worden. Mocht jij dus nog niet het track-record hebben dat in de vacature wordt gevraagd, maar wél solide werkervaring hebben, dan willen we jou ook graag uitnodigen om te solliciteren.

CONTRACT: 1 jaar

academictransfer:363514.description:
As an Environmental Technology Researcher at Wageningen Livestock Research, you will work on innovative solutions for emission reduction and sustainable manure processing. Please note that an excellent command of Dutch is essential for this role.
You will develop, investigate and support technological applications that contribute to a circular and future-proof livestock sector. Combining applied research with systems analysis, you will work both in the laboratory and on-site at livestock farms and manure processing facilities to test and optimise technologies under practical conditions. You will also use modelling and scenario analysis to assess the potential impact of new technologies at both regional and national levels.
 Your responsibilities as an Environmental Technology Researcher will include: 
 designing and carrying out research into manure processing technologies, including anaerobic digestion, drying and nutrient recovery from manure;
 measuring ammonia and greenhouse gas emissions from manure storage and processing systems;
 analysing and optimising environmental technologies used in livestock farming and advising on future technological developments;
 translating research findings into practical applications for the agricultural sector and government;
 collaborating with fellow researchers, industry partners, government bodies and other stakeholders;
 contributing to innovative projects focused on circular agriculture, renewable energy and sustainable farming.
 Your Team 
You will join a team of around 40 colleagues - including researchers, project leaders, measurement technicians and laboratory analysts - who are working together to develop innovative solutions for the livestock sector of the future. As a researcher, you will collaborate within the Emissions and Manure Valorisation multidisciplinary project teams.
Within the department, you will work alongside an enthusiastic group of approximately 40 colleagues with diverse expertise and responsibilities. The team also collaborates extensively with other parts of Wageningen University & Research and with a broad network of external partners.
You will be based within the Emissions and Manure Valorisation department of Livestock Research. 
 Our Mission 
The livestock sector faces significant challenges. Reducing ammonia and greenhouse gas emissions is essential for achieving a sustainable future and maintaining the sector's licence to operate. The Environmental Technology and Manure Valorisation team develops and evaluates technologies that reduce emissions and nutrient losses from manure. At the same time, we increasingly view animal manure as a valuable resource. By converting manure into renewable energy, supporting circular applications and developing new bio-based products, we contribute to a resilient agricultural sector that is less dependent on fossil resources.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are a proactive and results-oriented researcher who works effectively both independently and as part of multidisciplinary teams. You actively contribute to scientific impact by disseminating knowledge through publications, technical reports, and presentations at national and international conferences and symposia.
 You will also have: 
 a completed university degree in Environmental Technology, Process Engineering, Agricultural Engineering, Animal Sciences, or a related discipline;
 experience with manure processing technologies and applied research into technological innovations in livestock production;
 a strong interest in emission reduction, circular agriculture and sustainable farming systems;
 an excellent command of both Dutch and English.

CONTRACT: 1 year

academictransfer:363519.description:
In this PhD project you will investigate how Influenza A viruses bind and adapt to host glycans. You will combine glycan-based tools, molecular biology, and biochemical or analytical approaches to identify and characterise glycan receptors that drive influenza host and tissue tropism. We explicitly welcome candidates with backgrounds in glycoscience, molecular biology, biochemistry, enzymology, who are excited to work on virus–host interactions.
 Your job 
You will study how Influenza A viruses interact with complex glycans and how receptor binding evolves across strains and host species. Depending on your background and interests, you will contribute through complementary approaches such as:
 molecular and cell-based assays of influenza receptor binding;
 enzymatic modification or biosynthesis of glycans;
 biochemical characterisation of virus–glycan interactions.
These insights will be integrated to define glycan features that govern influenza attachment and to develop advanced in vitro receptor models reflecting airway tissues.
Your tools:
 Interdisciplinary receptor analysis: you will work with glycan arrays, engineered glycans, influenza binding assays, and—depending on expertise—molecular biology, enzymology, biochemical assays.
 Collaborative environment: you will join a multidisciplinary team spanning chemical biology, glycoscience, and virology, with close collaborations with national influenza researchers.
Your impact:
 Fundamental insight: you will uncover how Influenza A viruses adapt receptor binding to different hosts and tissues, advancing understanding of influenza evolution and host specificity.
 Preparedness: you will help establish physiologically relevant glycan-based receptor : models to support pandemic risk assessment and antiviral strategies.

REQUIREMENTS:
We are looking for a candidate who can start in 2027 and brings:
 a completed Master's degree in Molecular Biology, Biochemistry, Chemical Biology, Glycoscience, Virology, or a related field;
 hands-on experience in at least one of the following areas: molecular/cell biology, biochemical or enzymatic assays, glycan or protein analysis;
 interest in virus-host interactions;
 experience with influenza, glycans, or receptor-binding assays (ELISA, microscopy, FACS, arrays);
 strong communication skills in English, both written and verbal.

academictransfer:363526.description:
As a Saltmarsh Ecology Researcher, you will contribute to the saltmarsh research programme at Wageningen Marine Research (WMR). Working closely with the saltmarsh team, you will carry out field measurements and analyses to investigate long-term vegetation dynamics and sediment accretion, the impacts of land subsidence caused by gas extraction, and carbon sequestration in saltmarsh ecosystems. You will translate this knowledge into reports and provide advice on saltmarsh restoration plans and management strategies. In doing so, you will also contribute to the development of nature-based solutions for coastal areas and to understanding how these can be implemented in an ecologically responsible manner.
You will work with colleagues on several projects simultaneously, with responsibilities allocated according to capacity and individual interests. Engagement with clients and other stakeholders is an integral part of our work.
 Your responsibilities as a Saltmarsh Ecology Researcher include: 
 carrying out annual monitoring of sediment accretion and vegetation development across various saltmarshes in the Wadden Sea;
 analysing and interpreting monitoring data;
 writing reports for clients;
 assisting in the acquisition of new projects and the preparation of research proposals;
 publishing and presenting your research in scientific journals and at conferences.
 Wageningen Marine Research 
Your primary area of work will be the Dutch Wadden Sea, although opportunities may also arise to contribute to projects elsewhere in the Netherlands and internationally. Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) is a leading organisation in the Netherlands in the field of marine ecological data collection, covering benthic fauna, marine mammals, fish, birds, coral, shellfish, and more. Many of these datasets have been collected and maintained using standardised methodologies for decades. In this role, you will contribute to the continuation and development of the saltmarsh monitoring dataset, which contains more than 60 years of valuable ecological data.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are an enterprising researcher with expertise in the ecology of saltmarshes and saline vegetation communities, and a broad interest in the Wadden Sea and coastal ecosystems. You are able to work effectively under time pressure and manage your workload to meet deadlines. You are motivated, proactive, collaborative, client-focused, and possess strong communication skills.
 In addition, you have: 
 a completed relevant academic degree in the Natural or Life Sciences, with a specialisation in Wadden Sea and coastal ecosystems. A PhD in a field relevant to this position would be an advantage;
 knowledge of saltmarsh vegetation;
 experience with nature-based solutions;
 strong report-writing skills;
 experience in applying statistical analyses to long-term and spatial datasets;
 a full Category B driving licence;
 proficiency in Dutch would be an advantage.
In our international working environment there is an increasing amount of communication in English. For this position it is about language level C1.

CONTRACT: 1 year

academictransfer:363540.description:
Are you interested in the long‑term consequences of climate change and adaptation choices and the role that insurers and other financial institutions? Do you want to develop macroeconomic models that reveal where the tipping points lie in the relationship between changes in flood risk, financial markets and possible response strategies as protect, retreat and living with waterclimate adaption?
 Are you our new colleague? 
We are looking for a PhD candidate to build a macroeconomic model that links long‑term strategies for flood risk management to the behaviour of the financial sector and the broader economic development of the Netherlands. In this project, you will work on methods to quantify how choices regarding possible long‑term solutions – as distinguished by the IPCC (from protection to retreat and from adaptive building to spatial dispersal policies) – feed through to interest rate premia for a country on the capital market, capital requirements for banks and insurers, the investment climate and economic growth. You will study tipping points for the financial sector, given the evolution of flood probabilities and impacts.
About FIRM
Flood Insurance and Risk Management (FIRM) is a five‑year research programme (2026–2030), funded by TKI Deltatechnologie, Nationale‑Nederlanden and contributions from more than 10 partners from academia, the financial sector, engineering consultancies and government. The central aim of FIRM is to estimate flood and water nuisance risk information as realistically as possible and to make it suitable for use in the financial sector.
FIRM consists of four substantive research pillars and a work package on knowledge transfer. This vacancy concerns a PhD position within Pillar 2. You will collaborate closely with the PhD candidates in the other pillars and will be supervised by researchers from the University of Amsterdam and Deltares, working together with experts from the field.
 This is what you will be doing 
This PhD project focuses on the interaction between long‑term strategies for flood risk management, the behaviour of the financial sector and the macroeconomic development of the Netherlands. The project develops a methodology to make this interaction explicit. The research consists of:
 Further developing a macroeconomic model that incorporates the response of insurers and other financial institutions to climate change and possible long‑term adaptation strategies to reduce floodrisk (using the flood risk models of Pillar 1), including GDP effects and distributional effects between areas inside and outside transition zones;
 Identifying tipping points at which climate and transition risks because of climate adaptation influences investment choices;
 Developing and analyzing adaptation pathways: combinations of prevention, spatial planning and financial instruments that can postpone or avoid tipping points, assessed against the long‑term scenarios from the Sea Level Rise Knowledge Programme (Kennisprogramma Zeespiegelstijging, KP ZSS) and possible transition pathways based on the IPCC;
 Applying the methodology to case studies in the Netherlands in collaboration with project partners, and exploring international applications.
 Main duties and responsibilities: 
 Further developing a macroeconomic model that, in addition to protection, links other long‑term strategies to the behaviour of the financial sector and the implications for economic growth, including distributional effects between regions with different policies;
 Identifying tipping points at which climate and transition risks cause the financial sector to shift from a facilitating to a constraining role in spatial development;
 Developing adaptation pathways and assessing their effectiveness in terms of costs, benefits and distributional effects;
 Applying the model in workshops with insurers (led by Nationale‑Nederlanden) and policymakers (Ministry of Infrastructure and Water Management, Min. LnW; NLAAA);
 Presenting at international conferences and publishing in leading peer‑reviewed journals;
 Completing a PhD thesis within four years;
 Contributing to teaching activities (approximately 20% of your time), including tutorials and supervision of theses.
 This is what we ask of you 
This is an interdisciplinary project at the intersection of environmental economics, macroeconomics, actuarial science and water management. We realize that candidates will usually have expertise in one of these fields and ask for a genuine interest in the others.
 A Master’s degree (or completion before September 2026) in Economics, Econometrics, Actuarial Science, Applied Mathematics, Environmental Sciences or a related field;
 Strong quantitative background: familiarity with dynamic optimization, cost‑benefit analysis or macroeconomic modelling is an advantage;
 Experience with programming (Python, R, MATLAB or similar);
 Ability to collaborate constructively with stakeholders from the financial sector and government;
 Interest

academictransfer:363542.description:
Do you enjoy working in an interdisciplinary research environment? Are you interested in applying multiple methods to advance research in linguistics and cognitive science? If you imagine yourself in a PhD position at the crossroads of lab-based experimental linguistics and linguistic fieldwork, then we welcome your application!
 Evolution and processing of languages with late negation 
In this project you work in a small international team of (psycho)linguists and cognitive scientists on the evolution and processing of negation in language. Specifically, we investigate why most languages prefer to express the negator (‘not’) early rather than late in the utterance. You will learn to use multiple methods, including artificial language learning and EEG, collecting data from different language communities, including indigenous language speakers in Ecuador.
 This is what you will be doing 
 submission of a PhD thesis within the period of appointment;
 presenting intermediate research results at workshops and conferences;
 participation in the Research School and Faculty of Humanities PhD training programmes;
 (co-)teaching courses at BA-level in the second and third year of the appointment (maximum 0,2 fte per year).
 conduct field-based experiments in Ecuador
 organising knowledge dissemination activities;
 participating in meetings and training sessions of the project research group.
 This is what we ask of you 
 a completed Master's degree in Linguistics, Cognitive Science, Psychology or similar;
 excellent research and academic English skills demonstrated by a Master thesis.
 strong interest in fieldwork/field-based experimental methods
 (minimally) conversational proficiency in Spanish
 a strong cooperative attitude and willingness to engage in collaborative researchI
 Please note that if you already hold a doctorate/PhD or are working towards obtaining a similar degree elsewhere, you will not be admitted to a doctoral programme at the UvA. 
 Preferred additional qualifications: 
Previous experience with experimental methods (artificial language learning, behavioural psycholinguistics, EEG).
 This is what else we offer you 
 We offer a temporary employment contract for the period of 48 months. The first contract will be for 16 months, with an extension for the following 32 months, contingent on a positive performance evaluation within the first 12 months. The preferred but negotiable starting date is 01 January 2027;
 Based on a full-time appointment (38 hours per week) the gross monthly salary will range from €3.204 in the first year to €4.051 (scale P) in the last year. This does not include 8% holiday allowance and 8,3% year-end allowance. The UFO profile PhD candidate applicable. The Collective Labour Agreement of Universities of the Netherlands is applicable;
 PhD candidates receive a tuition fee waiver;
 PhD candidates have free access to courses offered by the Graduate School of Humanities and the Dutch National Research Schools ;
 Training in (field-based) EEG methods by the research team
 Excellent possibilities for further professional development and education;
 An inspiring academic and international work environment in the heart of Amsterdam;
 An enthusiastic and professional academic team.
 This is where you will be working 
This PhD position is part of an NWO-funded Open Competition Large project, led by two PIs: Eva van Lier and Marieke Schouwstra, affiliated with two research schools: the Amsterdam Center for Language and Communication (ACLC) and the Institute for Logic, Language and Computation (ILLC). You will join the PhD community of the Amsterdam Center for Language and Communication. In addition, our research team includes international specialists in psycholinguistics (Sebastian Sauppe, Elisabeth Norcliffe) and a postdoc in linguistic typology.
 Questions? 
For questions about the position or department, you can contact us during office hours at:
Eva van Lier ( e.h.vanlier@uva.nl ) and/or Marieke Schouwstra ( m.schouwstra@uva.nl )
 Application 
If you recognize yourself in the profile and are interested in the position, we look forward to receiving your application.
Your application should include the following information:
 a letter cover letter and full academic CV;
 a list of all Master-level modules you have taken, with an official transcript of grades;
 a writing sample (preferably your MA thesis);
 the names and email addresses of two references, including your advisor, who may be approached by the selection committee.
The vacancy closes on 12 October 2026. The first round of interviews will take place on 26-27 October 2026.

academictransfer:363548.description:
Ben jij een zelfstandige, proactieve en servicegerichte persoonlijkheid? Werk je graag met mensen? Ben je goed in organiseren, coördineren en het uitvoeren van meerdere taken tegelijk? Ben je vriendelijk en ruimdenkend? Beheers je zowel het Nederlands als het Engels uitstekend? Dan is dit misschien wel de perfecte kans voor jou! 
Als management/office assistent(e) speel je een sleutelrol binnen onze groep. Je bent verantwoordelijk voor diverse administratieve en organisatorische processen en zorgt voor de praktische ondersteuning van een internationale groep docenten, wetenschappers en onderzoekers. Je bent hun eerste aanspreekpunt wanneer zij tegen logistieke uitdagingen aanlopen en wanneer zij professionele ondersteuning nodig hebben in zowel voorspelbare als onverwachte situaties.
Als allround ondersteuner heb je daarnaast affiniteit met het werken met softwaresystemen om administratieve en secretariële ondersteuning te bieden aan deze groeiende groep wetenschappers en promovendi binnen de leerstoelgroep Forest & Nature Conservation Policy.
 Je belangrijkste werkzaamheden zijn onder meer: 
 Organiseren en bijwonen van groepsvergaderingen, inclusief notuleren en verslaglegging;
 Verzorgen van formele correspondentie, archivering en databeheer;
 Bestellen van goederen en diensten die de groep nodig heeft;
 Ondersteunen van het onderwijsproces, waaronder studenteninschrijvingen en cijferregistratie;
 Ondersteunen van de onboarding van nieuwe collega's, waaronder promovendi;
 Ondersteunen van de leerstoelhouder bij het organiseren van reizen, afspraken en managementzaken;
 Ondersteunen van communicatieactiviteiten, zoals het beheer van de groepswebsite;
 Ondersteunen van collega's bij de organisatie van workshops, seminars en conferenties.
 Jouw team 
De leerstoelgroep Forest and Nature Conservation Policy (FNP) bestaat uit ongeveer 45 hoogleraren, docenten, postdoctorale onderzoekers en promovendi uit meer dan 20 verschillende landen. Zij zijn gevestigd in Nederland of daarbuiten. Je standplaats is Wageningen. De groep groeit nog steeds verder.

REQUIREMENTS:
HBO+ werk- en denkniveau; MBO+ is ook bespreekbaar wanneer je beschikt over ruime relevante werkervaring;
 Ervaring in een administratieve, secretariële of ondersteunende kantoorfunctie;
 Enige ervaring met sociale media, websites en nieuwsbrieven is een pré, maar geen vereiste;
 Sterke organisatorische vaardigheden en het vermogen om effectief prioriteiten te stellen;
 Het vermogen om verbanden te zien, vooruit te denken en flexibel en oplossingsgericht te handelen;
 Het vermogen om meerdere taken tegelijk uit te voeren en prioriteiten te stellen;
 Een sterk verantwoordelijkheidsgevoel en een hoge mate van integriteit;
 Een goede beheersing van zowel het Nederlands als het Engels ( niveau C1 ).
Wij zijn op zoek naar een collega die zich voor langere tijd aan onze groep wil verbinden. Deze functie biedt mogelijkheden voor groei en ontwikkeling. Daarom moedigen wij je van harte aan om te solliciteren als je enthousiast bent over deze functie, ook wanneer je nog niet volledig voldoet aan alle genoemde functie-eisen.

CONTRACT: 1 jaar

academictransfer:363553.description:
Are you an independent, pro-active, and service-oriented personality? Do you enjoy working with people? Are you good at organising, coordinating and managing multiple tasks? Are you friendly and open-minded? Are you proficient in both Dutch and English? If so, this could be the perfect opportunity for you! 
As a management office assistant, you will hold a key role in our group. You will be in charge of many administrative/organisation processes, taking care of the practical needs of an international group of lecturers, scientists and researchers. You will be their ‘go-to person’ when they run up against logistical problems and when they need professional support in predictable and unpredictable situations.
As an all-round assistant, you will also need to have an affinity with using software systems to provide administrative and secretarial support to this growing group of scientists and PhD students in the Forest & Nature Conservation Policy Group.
Your main tasks will include, but not limited to:
 Organise and attend group meetings, including note-taking and reporting;
 Handle formal correspondence, archiving, and data management;
 Ordering goods and services required by the group;
 Support university education in student enrolment and mark registration;
 Support the onboarding of new colleagues, including PhD students;
 Assist the chair holder in arranging his travels, appointments and management needs;
 Support communication activities such as the group’s website;
 Support colleagues in organising workshops, seminars and conferences.
 Your team 
The Forest and Nature Conservation Policy Group (FNP) is a team of some 45 professors, lecturers, post-doctoral researchers and PhD students from more than 20 different countries, located either in The Netherlands or abroad. Your working place will be in Wageningen. The team is still growing in numbers.

REQUIREMENTS:
To be successful in this role, you have:
 HBO+ level of working and thinking; MBO+ level is acceptable if you have extensive relevant experience;
 Experience in an administrative, secretarial or office support role;
 Some experience with social media, websites and newsletters is an advantage, but not a must;
 Strong organisational skills and the ability to set priorities effectively;
 The ability to see connections, think ahead, and act flexibly and solution-oriented;
 The ability to multitask and set priorities;
 A strong sense of responsibility and integrity;
 A good command of both Dutch and English. For this position, it is about language level C1
We are looking for a colleague with a long-term commitment to our group, in a position that will offer opportunities for growth and development; therefore, even if you do not fully meet all the requirements listed here but are enthusiastic about the position, we encourage you to apply.

academictransfer:363564.description:
As a laboratory technician, you will process plankton samples in the laboratory that have been collected during surveys at sea. These samples consist of fish eggs, larvae and fish gonads (reproductive organs). You will accurately record the results of these analyses in data templates and specific software applications.
 Your tasks and responsibilities as a plankton laboratory technician include: 
 sorting and analysing samples using microscopy and image analysis techniques;
 preparing histological sections;
 documenting and entering data and processing results.
 Team and location 
You will work alongside approximately four colleagues in a close and dedicated research team at Wageningen Marine Research based in IJmuiden. Together with this group of specialists, you will contribute to the successful delivery of a wide range of projects.
Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) is one of the nine research institutes that form part of Wageningen Research. WMR is a leading independent research institute with locations in IJmuiden, Den Helder and Yerseke, specialising in both strategic and applied marine ecological research. Its products and services are delivered through projects and cover a broad range of activities, including field studies, experiments at realistic scales, exploratory laboratory studies, data management and modelling.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are able to work effectively both as part of a team and independently. You work accurately and carefully, enabling you to handle samples and data reliably within our laboratory environment.
In addition, you have:
 a completed MBO level 4 qualification or equivalent vocational qualification. Candidates with an academic background will not be considered for this position;
 an interest in working with the smallest organisms found in the marine environment;
 demonstrable affinity with microscopy work;
 proficiency in the Dutch language ( minimum level B2 );
 laboratory experience and a basic proficiency of the English language (A2 level) are considered an advantage.

CONTRACT: 1 year

academictransfer:363646.description:
Ben jij een servicegerichte systeembeheerder met een passie voor innovatie die ook een gebruiker kan ondersteunen? Wil jij werken in een inspirerende wetenschappelijke omgeving waar je bijdraagt aan baanbrekend ecologisch onderzoek? Dan zijn wij op zoek naar jou!
Het Nederlands Instituut voor Ecologie (NIOO-KNAW) is een gerenommeerd onderzoeksinstituut dat zich richt op ecologisch en biodiversiteitsonderzoek. Voor de ondersteuning van onze onderzoekers beheren wij ons eigen datacenter en IT-infrastructuur.
 Dit ga je doen 
Als ICT Beheerder ben je samen met de collega’s van de sectie ICT verantwoordelijk voor het beheer van meerdere servers en opslagsystemen draaiend op verschillende besturingssystemen. Je zorgt ervoor dat onze IT-infrastructuur optimaal functioneert en ondersteunt het onderzoek met stabiele en veilige systemen.
Jouw taken omvatten onder andere:
 Beheer en onderhoud van servers en storage-oplossingen;
 Uitvoeren van reguliere updates en systeemvervangingen;
 Afhandelen van incidenten en uitvoeren van aanpassingen;
 Actief bijdragen aan verbetering.

REQUIREMENTS:
Dit breng je mee 
Wij zoeken een proactieve beheerder met een hands-on mentaliteit.
Daarnaast beschik je over:
 Een afgeronde opleiding in ICT-beheer op HBO werk- en denkniveau;
 Aantoonbare werkervaring met Windows en Linux systeembeheer;
 Ervaring met Cisco netwerkapparatuur is een pré;
 Een flexibele en dienstverlenende instelling, waarbij je zowel zelfstandig als in teamverband goed functioneert;
 Goede communicatieve vaardigheden in het Nederlands (minimaal level C1) en Engels.

academictransfer:363679.description:
Utrecht University School of Economics (U.S.E.) is a young and growing Economics department. The position of the Chair in Banking and Finance contributes to the (inter)national reputation and profile of U.S.E. in line with its multidisciplinary vision on research, teaching, and impact in the area of banking and finance. The Chair plays a crucial role in strengthening the international research profile and expertise of U.S.E. and its Finance section.
 Wat ga je doen? 
The main goal of the Chair in Banking and Finance is to further advance research (aligned with the relevant UU strategic themes, mainly “ Institutions for Open Societies ” and/or “ Pathways to Sustainability ”), and education in the relevant fields of expertise. Apart from their own research, we expect the Chair to play a stimulating role in the USE research community; to promote a research environment in line with USE’s aims for Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion; and to attract external research funding through grants and/or consulting. Additional duties include supervision of PhD Candidates and mentoring of (Post-Doctoral) researchers resulting in state-of-the-art PhD-manuscripts and publications, and the transfer of knowledge to society by organizing events (such as conferences and workshops). Teaching of the chair will connect the field with other relevant disciplines, in line with U.S.E.’s Real World Perspective. Cooperation with other USE sections and other departments and faculties within UU in teaching and research is strongly encouraged. The tasks and responsibilities of the Chair will include:
 Performing and encouraging scientific research in the field of Banking and Finance;
 Supervision of PhD students and post-Doctoral researchers;
 Participating in education in the U.S.E. Bachelor and Master programs, as well as executive education;
 Leadership in contributing to the research and teaching climate, including stimulating and supervising multidisciplinary research and teaching;
 Contributing to the financial sustainability of U.S.E., including active participation in external fundraising.

REQUIREMENTS:
The person we are looking for is a passionate and creative academic with a strong background in finance and a genuine interest in Banking and Finance. Given the current developments in this field, knowledge/research on digital/data science and AI, and the impact upon Finance – including its role for enabling societal transitions - is highly valued. We expect the chair to have a vision on the impact of data and societal transitions on finance and the financial industry. The person should be able to integrate digital knowledge in courses in the academic Finance master programs.
The ideal candidate for this position has a strong publication record and teaching experience in the field of Banking and/or Finance. The candidate is also willing and able to reach out to researchers active in other fields, such as economics and management. This requires an open and inquisitive mind, and a willingness to experiment and explore new avenues and methods in research and academic teaching. We anticipate that the candidate is willing and able to communicate complex scientific results and insights to an audience of policy makers, societal stakeholders, and the wider public; proven ability in this respect is considered a plus.
The following profile will be expected of the chair holder:
 A doctorate in the field of finance or economics;
 A strong track record in research, as demonstrated among others by publications in internationally recognised journals, a high number of citations, and preferably membership of policy advisor bodies (e.g. CEPR, NBER, etc);
 Impact in the field, as demonstrated by e.g. editiorial roles, conference organisation, invited talks or keynote lectures, engagement with policy or professional organisations, or other forms of societal or academic impact;
 An interest in and commitment to multidisciplinary research and collaboration across disciplinary boundaries;
 Significant experience in academic leadership, demonstrated, for example, through leadership roles, the development of teams or research programmes, mentoring and supporting colleagues, or other contributions to an inclusive, socially safe and collaborative academic environment, together with a clear vision on academic leadership;
 Enthusiasm and commitment to the further development of research and teaching in banking and/or Finance from a multidisciplinary perspective within UU;
 Ability to acquire external funding;
 Good communication skills;
 Fluency in English; ability to communicate in Dutch is preferred.

academictransfer:363722.description:
Welcome to Maastricht University! 
Help us better understand endometrial physiology, diseases and cancer, and contribute to new treatment strategies.
Are you an enthusiastic research technician with an educational background in biomedical sciences, molecular biology, biotechnology, life sciences or a related field? Do you enjoy working with advanced laboratory models and collaborating with researchers and clinicians from different disciplines? Then we would like to hear from you.
 Research Technician – Endometrial Biology and Gynecological Cancer Research 
 Our goal: You will join our translational research team, which investigates endometrial biology and Gynaecological diseases, with a particular focus on endometrial cancer. Our research focuses on the role of steroid hormones in gynaecological diseases, including the therapeutic potential of hormonal treatments, mechanisms of treatment resistance and the identification of new therapeutic targets and drug combinations. To address these questions, we combine advanced in vitro and in vivo models with analyses of patient-derived materials.
 Your colleagues: a research team consisting of a principal investigator, senior scientists, research technicians, PhD candidates and undergraduate and master's students. We work closely with researchers from different disciplines and with clinical partners.
Our translational research environment brings together clinicians, molecular biologists, bioinformaticians, data scientists and bioengineers. You will also have the opportunity to interact with national and international research collaborators.
Our research environment provides access to a wide range of advanced facilities and technologies, including:
 animal facilities and in vivo imaging platforms, including CT, PET-CT and bioluminescence imaging;
 advanced organoid and cell culture facilities;
 facilities for working with transgenic materials;
 advanced microscopy, including confocal, electron and two-photon microscopy;
 genomic and transcriptomic technologies, including bulk, single-cell and spatial analyses;
 mass spectrometry and mass spectrometry imaging;
 flow cytometry.
This gives you the opportunity to work with a broad range of techniques and to further develop your technical and scientific skills.
 What you do 
As a Research Technician, you will contribute to several ongoing research projects. You will work closely with PhD candidates, postdoctoral researchers, senior scientists and clinicians in a collaborative and multidisciplinary environment.
Your main responsibilities include:
 establishing, maintaining and characterising patient-derived organoid models of endometrial tissue and endometrial cancer;
 supporting research into steroid hormone metabolism in healthy and malignant endometrial tissues;
 assisting with the development, maintenance and experimental use of mouse models for endometrial cancer;
 performing molecular and cellular biology techniques, including RNA and DNA isolation, qPCR, protein analyses, immunohistochemistry, immunofluorescence and tissue processing;
 collecting, processing and managing patient-derived samples and biobank materials;
 supporting the day-to-day organisation and operation of the laboratory.
Depending on your experience and interests, you may also have the opportunity to develop your expertise in new techniques and contribute to different projects within the research.
 Are you ready to set the course for the years ahead? Then we’d love to meet you. 
 What you bring 
We are looking for a motivated and curious colleague who is enthusiastic about biomedical research and enjoys working in a multidisciplinary team.
You have:
 a completed HBO or Bachelor's degree, or equivalent training, in Biomedical Sciences, Molecular Biology, Biotechnology, Life Sciences, or a related discipline;
 experience with cell culture and basic molecular biology techniques;
 good communication skills in English and teamwork skills;
 the ability to work independently and take responsibility for your work;
 a proactive and flexible attitude;
 an interest in contributing to translational cancer research.
It would be an advantage if you also have:
 experience with three-dimensional culture systems, such as organoids or spheroids;
 experience with animal experimentation and mouse handling;
 experience with histological or imaging techniques;
 an interest in endocrinology and gynaecological oncology.
You do not need to meet every preferred qualification. If you are motivated to learn and develop your skills, we encourage you to apply.
 What we offer 
At Maastricht University, you’ll work in an international, open, and engaged environment. We offer:
 A 12-month contract (0,4 fte) with the prospect of a permanent contract based on mutual satisfaction.
 A gross monthly salary between €3537,00 and €4673,00 (based on full-time employment of 38 hours per week). 8% holiday allowance and an 8.3% year-end bonus.
 29 vacation days (based on full-time), four addi

academictransfer:363755.description:
This is an exciting opportunity to combine advanced specialist training with diagnostic and clinical activities, teaching, and research. You will follow a structured training programme of approximately four years. Training will take place under the supervision of experienced specialists and scientists and an EVPC Diplomate (located at Ghent University).
 Your job 
As an EVPC Resident, you will complete an individual four-year training programme designed to provide broad and in-depth expertise in veterinary parasitology. https://www.eurovetpar.org/residencyprograms 
Your training will take place within the Faculty of Veterinary Medicine at Utrecht University in collaboration with Ghent University in Belgium and will include diagnostic, clinical, educational aspects of veterinary parasitology, and research. Externships and training in other institutions such as Wageningen University and Research, Royal Animal Health, Dutch Wildlife Health Centre, National Institute for Public Health and the Environment will be part of the programme.
About forty percent of your work will be dedicated to teaching. You will teach veterinary bachelor and master students and will have the opportunity to develop your teaching skills (University Teaching Qualification, https://www.uu.nl/en/education/centre-for-academic-teaching-and-learning/teacher-development/utq-and-stq ).
You will develop your expertise in different aspects of veterinary parasitology including clinical training, and diagnostic practice at e.g. the Veterinary Microbiological Diagnostic Centre of Utrecht University, and research. Topics of interest will involve laboratory diagnostics, epidemiology, host–parasite interactions, zoonotic parasites, parasite control, and antiparasitic stewardship.
The training programme will be tailored to ensure broad exposure to different aspects of the EVPC curriculum.
As a Resident in training in Veterinary Parasitology, you will:
 complete an EVPC residency training programme;
 participate in veterinary parasitology diagnostics at the Veterinary Microbiological Diagnostic Centre (VMDC) and the Veterinary Pathology Diagnostic Centre (VPDC) and discuss the interpretation of the diagnostic results with clinicians of the faculty clinics and veterinary practices in the Netherlands;
 conduct research on one of the research topics of the group (Anthelmintic Stewardship, parasitological (molecular) diagnostics) and prepare case reports leading to scientific peer-reviewed publications;
 contribute to veterinary parasitology teaching within our bachelor and master programme and share your knowledge on parasitology with (para)veterinarians, farmers, pet owners, and general public;
 participate in national and international meetings, conferences, courses and continuing education activities;
 work collaboratively with national and international colleagues from veterinary medicine, veterinary public health, biomedical sciences and related disciplines;

REQUIREMENTS:
We are looking for a veterinarian who is highly motivated to pursue specialist training in veterinary parasitology and who enjoys combining research, clinical and diagnostic activities with teaching.
You have:
 a European degree in Veterinary Medicine (approved by the EAEVE); preferably a PhD;
 a strong interest in veterinary parasitology and a clear motivation to pursue an EVPC residency;
 gained at least one year of experience in the veterinary practice and/or in a diagnostic or research laboratory;
 an enthusiastic demeanour regarding veterinary parasitology teaching and outreach;
 good communication skills that will help function in a multidisciplinary and international environment;
 excellent written and spoken English;
 while teaching is conducted in Dutch, candidates who are not yet proficient in Dutch are expected to be willing to learn the language within two years.
Previous experience in veterinary parasitology, diagnostic laboratory work, research, and/or teaching is desirable. Experience with scientific writing and data analysis would also be an advantage.
Applicants must meet the applicable requirements for admission to an EVPC residency program (Alternative Residency and Training Programme in collaboration with Ghent University). The residency programme is subject to approval and supervision in accordance with current EVPC requirements.

academictransfer:363761.description:
Do you work in the field of observational massive star and compact object binary science? Are you interested in contributing to a diverse and collaborative research and teaching community?
 Join us! 
The Anton Pannekoek Institute for Astronomy (API) of the University of Amsterdam (UvA) is seeking to hire an assistant professor in high-energy astrophysics. We especially welcome applications from experts in multi-wavelength and time-domain observational astronomy that connects to new and future facilities such as SKA, CTA, NewAthena, and ELT. The candidate will lead a research program focused on the roles of binary star systems with massive stars, stellar-mass black holes and neutron stars, as laboratories for binary evolution, extreme gravity, accretion, particle acceleration and feedback.
 This is what you will do 
 Conducting independent research in high-energy astrophysics, resulting in academic publications in peer-reviewed international journals;
 Building and leading a research group, in the spirit of team science;
 Actively pursuing external funding;
 Actively contributing to the development of national and international research networks;
 Actively contributing to the governance and improvement of our broader teaching programmes;
 Taking part in committees and carrying out departmental administrative tasks;
 Supervising students of all levels and postdocs;
 Contributing to and actively seeking opportunities to increase the societal impact and visibility of research through media engagement, public lectures, or collaborative educational initiatives.
 What we ask of you 
 A relevant PhD degree and preferably postdoctoral experience;
 Outstanding research skills in observational high-energy astrophysics, evidenced by a strong publication track record, and a clear vision for the advertised research profile;
 Proven ability or potential to lead a research group and mentor students across different academic levels and of diverse backgrounds;
 A strong interest in, and preferably experience with, science that complements the API’s strategic involvement in upcoming facilities;
 Potential in attracting external funding and resources;
 Affinity for teaching at Bachelor and Master level, evidenced by previous teaching efforts and/or a clearly articulated teaching philosophy;
 Management skills required for general academic administration and organisation, demonstrated through participation in committees, panels, working groups, etc.;
 Affinity for outreach activities, reflected in participation in previous initiatives and future plans;
 The Dutch University Teaching Qualification (UTQ/BKO) or willingness to acquire this within the first two years;
 Good command of English and strong commitment to learn the Dutch language via a supported learning trajectory.
 Our offer 
We offer a temporary employment contract for 38 hours per week with a tenure track agreement for the duration of 5 years, ending at the level of associate professor. The arrangement is such that after a maximum of 3 years and positive assessment and evaluation you will be offered a permanent position while the track continues towards the level of associate professor. Details of the criteria will be mutually agreed upon before the start of the contract in the Tenure Track agreement. The preferred starting date is as soon as possible.
The position comes with a start-up package, including funding for a PhD student, relocation support, mentoring, and leadership training.
The gross monthly salary, based on 38 hours per week and dependent on relevant experience, ranges between € 4,925 to € 6,682 (scale 11). This does not include 8% holiday allowance and 8,3% year-end allowance. The UFO profile UD2 is applicable. A favourable tax agreement, the ‘30% ruling’, may apply to non-Dutch applicants. The Collective Labour Agreement of Universities of the Netherlands is applicable.
Besides the salary and a vibrant and stimulating environment at Science Park, we offer you multiple fringe benefits:
 232 holiday hours per year (based on full-time) and extra holidays between Christmas and 1 January;
 multiple courses to follow from our Teaching and Learning Centre;
 multiple courses on topics such as leadership for academic staff;
 multiple courses on topics such as time management, handling stress and an online learning platform with100+ different courses;
 7 weeks birth leave (partner leave) with 100% salary;
 partly paid parental leave;
 the possibility to set up a workplace at home;
 a pension at ABP for which UvA pays two third part of the contribution;
 the possibility to follow courses to learn Dutch.
 Where you will work 
The Anton Pannekoek Institute has a long tradition of excellence in astronomical research and is part of the Dutch Top Research School NOVA ( http://www.nova-astronomy.nl ). API scientists have stablished a thriving interdisciplinary collaborative research and training program with the GRAPPA (Gravitational Radiation and Astro Particle Phys

academictransfer:363789.description:
In this project, you will investigate how schema-driven approaches can improve the interoperability, discoverability, and reuse of biomedical data resources. Many biomedical databases contain valuable information, but their schemas, metadata, access methods, and documentation are often heterogeneous, making integration and reuse challenging.
You will focus on analyzing and harmonizing database schemas, enriching metadata, and aligning biomedical resources with relevant standards, ontologies, and community practices. The project will initially focus on prioritized biomedical databases in the Rare Disease and Proteomics domains.
The research will contribute to more transparent and reproducible integration of biomedical databases by connecting schema information to FAIR metadata, semantic annotations, and community platforms such as BioDataFuse, bio.tools, and Galaxy. Through this work, you will help strengthen the FAIR and open science ecosystem for biomedical research.
We offer a two-year postdoctoral position in a new open science infrastructure project with direct relevance for the biomedical research community. You will have the opportunity to contribute to practical FAIR data solutions that support the interoperability, discoverability, and reuse of biomedical databases.
As a postdoc in FAIR biomedical data interoperability, you will play a central role in developing and implementing approaches for schema analysis, schema harmonization, and metadata integration across biomedical databases. You will work in a dynamic, collaborative environment at the intersection of bioinformatics, FAIR data stewardship, semantic data integration, and research infrastructure development.
Your primary responsibilities will include:
 You will help identify and analyze the schema of biomedical databases, particularly in the Rare Disease and Proteomics domains, examining their structure, metadata, and access methods to support interoperability and reuse;
 You will contribute to mapping database schemas to established standards and ontologies, including Bioschemas, EDAM, OBO Foundry resources, and related semantic technologies;
 You will help generate structured schema descriptions and enriched FAIR metadata to improve the discoverability, accessibility, and usability of biomedical databases;
 You will contribute to integrating schema information into platforms such as BioDataFuse, bio.tools, and Galaxy, thereby enabling more reusable and interoperable data analysis workflows;
 You will help ensure that project outputs follow FAIR and open science principles, and contribute to documentation, dissemination, and adoption by the wider research community;
 You will work closely with project partners, database providers, and user communities, including stakeholders in ELIXIR and national infrastructure initiatives, to align project outputs with community needs;
 You will contribute to scientific publications and presentations at national and international events, helping to disseminate the project’s outputs and impact.

REQUIREMENTS:
Required qualifications for this position are:
 a PhD in bioinformatics, computational biology, data science, biomedical informatics, or a related discipline;
 experience with biomedical data integration, data modelling, or metadata standards;
 knowledge of FAIR data principles and open science practices;
 strong organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills;
 strong written and oral communication skills in English;
 ability to work independently as well as collaboratively in interdisciplinary and multi-institutional settings;
 a high attention to detail and a commitment to delivering high-quality results.
Preferred experience:
 with semantic web technologies, ontologies, RDF, or schema languages such as ShEx;
 with metadata standards such as Bioschemas, EDAM, or related life-science standards;
 with biomedical databases, knowledge graphs, or schema-driven data integration;
 with platforms such as bio.tools, Galaxy, WorkflowHub, or similar community resources.

academictransfer:363807.description:
Wil jij zorgen dat cliënten en verwijzend dierenartsen snel op de juiste plek terechtkomen? Bij het Universitair Dierenziekenhuis combineer je klantcontact met plannen, regelen en patiëntenadministratie. Je werkt op een unieke plek waar specialistische diergeneeskundige zorg, onderwijs en wetenschappelijk onderzoek samenkomen.
 Wat ga je doen? 
Als medewerker klantcontact en patiëntenadministratie ben je een belangrijk aanspreekpunt voor cliënten en verwijzend dierenartsen van de Kliniek voor Gezelschapsdieren. Je werkt op verschillende onderdelen van de patiëntenadministratie, waaronder telefonie en receptie. Daarnaast ondersteunt de afdeling onder meer het voedingsinformatiecentrum, casemanagement en facturatie. Je hebt daardoor veel contact met dierenartsen, specialistische afdelingen en andere collega’s binnen de kliniek.
Aan de telefoon en via e-mail beantwoord je uiteenlopende vragen. Je schat in wat een vraag nodig heeft, bepaalt wat voorrang heeft en zorgt dat informatie en acties bij de juiste persoon terechtkomen. Soms geef je zelf direct antwoord. Je hoeft daarbij niet alles zelf te weten: bij complexe vragen overleg je met een collega of dierenarts. Daarnaast plan en coördineer je afspraken bij de specialistische afdelingen.
Bij de receptie ontvang je cliënten met hun dier. Je zorgt voor een gastvrije ontvangst, controleert en completeert de inschrijving en helpt cliënten met praktische vragen. Je werkzaamheden bestaan onder andere uit:
 beantwoorden van vragen van cliënten en verwijzend dierenartsen via telefoon en e-mail;
 inschatten van de aard en urgentie van vragen en bepalen welke vervolgstap nodig is;
 plannen en coördineren van afspraken en verstrekken van relevante informatie;
 ontvangen en aanmelden van cliënten met hun dier bij de receptie;
 verwerken en prioriteren van e-mails, informatie en gegevens in de patiëntenadministratie;
 afhandelen van aanvragen via de spoedtelefoon en servicelijn voor verwijzend dierenartsen.
Je werkt in een team van ongeveer 25 vaste collega’s, aangevuld met flexibele collega’s. Jullie werken nauw samen en stemmen gedurende de dag veel met elkaar af. Ook heb je veel contact met dierenartsen, specialistische afdelingen en andere teams binnen het Universitair Dierenziekenhuis. Zo zorgen jullie er samen voor dat cliënten en verwijzend dierenartsen goed worden geholpen en de administratieve processen rondom de patiëntenzorg goed verlopen.

REQUIREMENTS:
Je kunt goed organiseren en regelen. Je houdt overzicht over afspraken, vragen en acties en zorgt dat zaken op het juiste moment bij de juiste persoon terechtkomen. Wanneer het druk is, bepaal je wat voorrang heeft en schakel je gemakkelijk tussen telefoon, e-mail, planning en administratie.
Je werkt in een omgeving waarin veel informatie binnenkomt. Je pakt nieuwe informatie snel op en verwerkt gegevens in een goed tempo én zorgvuldig. Je communiceert duidelijk met cliënten, verwijzend dierenartsen en collega’s en stemt actief af wanneer je anderen nodig hebt om een vraag verder te brengen.
 Voor deze functie is het belangrijk dat je: 
 goed kunt organiseren, coördineren en prioriteiten stellen;
 informatie en administratieve gegevens snel en nauwkeurig verwerkt en gemakkelijk met digitale systemen werkt;
 duidelijk communiceert, zowel telefonisch als schriftelijk;
 goed samenwerkt en actief afstemt met collega’s en andere afdelingen.
 Een achtergrond als paraveterinair of medisch secretaresse en kennis van medische terminologie kunnen helpen om je de werkzaamheden sneller eigen te maken, maar zijn geen vereiste. Je krijgt ruimte om te leren en je verder te ontwikkelen. Tegelijkertijd pak je nieuwe informatie snel op en leer je veel tijdens het werk, in een omgeving waarin verschillende vragen en werkzaamheden elkaar in hoog tempo afwisselen.

academictransfer:363857.description:
Bij de Universiteit Utrecht speelt onderwijs een belangrijke rol. Als docent bij de afdeling Ontwikkelingspsychologie maak je deel uit van een goed functionerend team van docenten en onderzoekers. Gezamenlijk wordt zorggedragen voor het geven van hooggekwalificeerd academisch onderwijs op het gebied van wetenschappelijk kennis en onderzoek (o.a. theorie, methoden) en klinische praktijkvaardigheden (o.a. diagnostiek, gespreksvoering, observaties). Docenten worden ondersteund in het ontwikkelen en innoveren van hun onderwijs.
 Wat ga je doen? 
Bij de afdeling Ontwikkelingspsychologie van de Faculteit Sociale Wetenschappen zoeken we een nieuwe collega voor het (klinisch) onderwijs dat deze afdeling verzorgt in de overwegend Nederlandstalige bacheloropleiding Psychologie en in de Engelstalige masteropleiding Clinical Child and Adolescent Psychology . De werkzaamheden bestaan zowel uit werkgroep onderwijs als individuele studentbegeleiding.
Het onderwijs van onze afdeling is gericht op het domein van de (klinische) ontwikkelingspsychologie, waarbinnen de normatieve en problematisch ontwikkeling van kindertijd tot jong volwassenheid aan bod komt, met onderwerpen als de sociaal-cognitieve en emotionele ontwikkeling, persoonlijkheid en identiteit. Daarnaast verzorgt de afdeling vakken gericht op onderzoeksmethoden- en vaardigheden, en kan je betrokken zijn bij thesis begeleiding. Afhankelijk van je expertise is er ook een mogelijkheid om onderwijs te geven op het gebied van psychodiagnostiek (methoden, besluitvorming) en betrokken te zijn bij begeleiding van studenten tijdens hun klinische stage.

REQUIREMENTS:
Jij:
 hebt een (research) master-diploma in de psychologie of een vergelijkbare master;
 bent bij voorkeur gepromoveerd;
 hebt ervaring en affiniteit met het geven van academisch onderwijs;
 bent in staat naast Nederlandstalig ook Engelstalig onderwijs te geven;
 bent in het bezit van de Basis Kwalificatie Onderwijs of bent bereid deze op termijn te behalen;
 hebt bij voorkeur ervaring met klinische werkzaamheden in de zorg voor kinderen en jeugdigen (bijv. diagnostiek en/of behandeling);
 bent enthousiast, flexibel, georganiseerd, stressbestendig en sociaal vaardig.

academictransfer:363865.description:
How does the microbiome surrounding a plant determine whether a pathogen can successfully invade? And can we predict disease outcomes from microbiome composition and plant phenotypes? Join us as a PhD candidate to investigate how the potato microbiome influences interactions with two major plant pathogens: Phytophthora infestans and Ralstonia solanacearum . You will combine high-throughput plant phenotyping, microbiome profiling, predictive modelling and targeted experimental validation to identify the microbial factors that make plants more resilient to disease.
 Your job 
You will join the Plant-Microbe Interactions group and work within the NWO Vici project ResistoBiome: Predictive microbiome modelling for resilient and sustainable potato cropping systems.
In this project, you will investigate why the same pathogen can cause severe disease in one microbial environment but establish poorly in another. You will work with a unique collection of 100 Dutch agricultural soil microbiomes and grow potato plants in the automated phenotyping facilities of the Netherlands Plant Eco-phenotyping Centre (NPEC) . Using high-resolution imaging, you will follow plant growth, physiology and disease development after infection with Phytophthora or Ralstonia .
You will combine these phenotyping data with bacterial and fungal microbiome profiles to build predictive models of pathogen invasion and plant performance. Working in close collaboration with with a postdoctoral researcher and a fellow PhD candidate, you will identify microbial taxa and microbial interactions that are most strongly associated with disease resistance or susceptibility.
A key part of your project will be to move from prediction to biological understanding. You will select promising microbes emerging from the models, isolate and experimentally test them, and investigate how they influence pathogen establishment and plant health. This will allow you to ask not only which microbes predict disease outcomes, but also why they matter.
During your PhD, you will:
 perform large-scale potato experiments using 100 contrasting soil microbiomes;
 use automated high-throughput phenotyping to quantify plant growth, physiology and disease development;
 profile root and leaf microbiomes using amplicon sequencing;
 analyse integrated microbiome and phenotyping datasets;
 contribute to machine-learning models predicting pathogen invasion success and plant resilience;
 identify microbial taxa and interactions associated with contrasting disease outcomes;
 isolate and experimentally validate selected candidate microbes;
 design follow-up experiments to understand how protective microbes influence plant–pathogen interactions;
 collaborate closely with researchers in microbiology, plant pathology, phenotyping, bioinformatics and predictive modelling.
The project gives you the opportunity to work across scales: from thousands of plants and complex microbiome datasets to mechanistic experiments with individual microbial isolates.

REQUIREMENTS:
You enjoy combining experimental biology with quantitative approaches and are eager to develop skills in microbiome data analysis and predictive modelling;
You have:
 an MSc degree in plant biology, microbiology, molecular biology, plant pathology, biotechnology, bioinformatics or a related discipline;
 experience with experimental laboratory work or plant experiments;
 a strong interest in plant–microbe interactions and microbiome research;
 an interest in quantitative biology, phenotyping and data analysis;
 the ability to organise research activities effectively, to communicate research findings to different audiences and collaborate across disciplines;
 proficiency in spoken and written English.
Experience with plant pathogens, microbiome sequencing, image-based phenotyping, R/Python, statistics or machine learning is an advantage. We do not expect candidates to already have expertise in all of these areas.

academictransfer:363888.description:
Do you care about the impacts of land use in ecologically sensitive regions of the world? Are motivated to explore how rearranging land use can minimize negative impacts and maximize the positive ones for different stakeholder groups, as a basis for policy making? Are you interested in spatial optimization algorithms and uncertainty assessment? Then this PhD position at the Department of Human Geography and Spatial Planning of Utrecht University may be for you!
 Your job 
The MATOPIBA region in the Brazilian Cerrado is of major national and global importance: it is one of South America’s most agriculturally productive landscapes, a biodiversity hotspot, and home to Indigenous people, Quilombola, smallholder farmers, and large agribusinesses among others. Therefore, future land use strategies in MATOPIBA require balancing agricultural production with biodiversity conservation and social justice, while also accounting for effects of climate change. In this position, you will contribute to such land use strategies by quantifying the synergies and trade-offs between agricultural production and biodiversity conservation for different land use configurations and stakeholder preferences. Hereto, you will compute Pareto frontiers with spatial optimization algorithms.
More specifically, you will:
 Travel to Brazil to interact with various stakeholders in land use in MATOPIBA;
 Translate stakeholders’ preferences in terms of ‘productive agriculture’ and ‘healthy biodiversity’ into quantifiable impact indicators;
 Develop a spatial optimization algorithm that finds land use configurations that optimize these indicators;
 Compute Pareto frontiers under different climate and socio-economic scenarios to evaluation the robustness of land use solutions for all stakeholder groups; and
 Write academic articles and policy documents that summarize the above to generate and disseminate knowledge on the possible pathways to sustainable land use futures in MATOPIBA.
This PhD position is part of the project “Frontiers of MATOPIBA landscapes”, which examines how agricultural production and biodiversity conservation can be balanced in Brazil’s MATOPIBA region under future climate and socio-economic conditions. It has the aim to support evidence-based decisions to form sustainable, just, climate-resilient land use strategies that protect biodiversity, food security, and local livelihoods. The project is fully funded through the Dutch Research Agenda and the Brazilian research council CNPq . Within the project, you will closely collaborate with another PhD candidate at Utrecht University on stakeholder perspectives, several consortium members in Brazil, and the Department of Sustainable Development at Utrecht University.

REQUIREMENTS:
We are looking for candidates with:
 An MSc degree in (or nearing completion of) an MSc program in Geoinformatics, Computer Science, (Quantitative) Geography, (Spatial / Geographic) Data Science, Environmental Science, or a related discipline;
 An interest in the impacts of land use;
 Experience in handling spatial data;
 Programming experience in Python, and/or R;
 Proficiency in English; and
 The ability to work as part of an interdisciplinary research team.
The following qualifications are desirable but not required:
 Experience with (spatial) optimization;
 Experience with land use (change) impact assessment; or
 Proficiency in Portuguese.

academictransfer:363889.description:
Do you care about impacts of land use on the livelihoods of various groups of people in ecologically sensitive regions of the world? Do you want to explore what kind of land use futures the people aspire? And are you eager to bring these perspectives together to co-produce promising pathways towards sustainable land use futures? Then this PhD position at the Department of Human Geography and Spatial Planning of Utrecht University may be for you!
 Your job 
The MATOPIBA region in the Brazilian Cerrado is of major national and global importance: it is one of South America’s most agriculturally productive landscapes, a biodiversity hotspot, and home to Indigenous people, Quilombola, smallholder farmers, and large agribusinesses among others. Therefore, future land use strategies in MATOPIBA require balancing agricultural production with biodiversity conservation and social justice, while also accounting for the effects of climate change. In this position, you will contribute to co-producing such land use strategies by interacting with the different stakeholder groups, including policy makers, to understand how agricultural production and biodiversity impact various people’s livelihoods. Methodologically, you will use qualitative methods such as field observations, focus group discussions, semi-structured interviews, and participatory/ co-creation of maps.
More specifically, you will:
 Travel to Brazil to conduct field research through which you interact with various actor groups and stakeholders such as policy makers and private sectors in land use in MATOPIBA;
 Discover their preferences in terms of ‘productive agriculture’ and ‘healthy biodiversity’ through focus groups and semi-structured interviews;
 Facilitate exercises of participatory mapping to co-create maps to identify desired land use futures for various groups;
 Further discuss and document the potential of alternative land use futures with the stakeholders in public, private, and civil society sectors; and
 Write academic articles and policy documents that summarize the above to generate and disseminate knowledge on the possible pathways to sustainable land use futures in MATOPIBA.
This PhD position is part of the project “Frontiers of MATOPIBA landscapes”, which examines how agricultural production and biodiversity conservation can be balanced in Brazil’s MATOPIBA region under future climate and socio-economic conditions. It has the aim to support evidence-based decisions to form sustainable, just, climate-resilient land use strategies that protect biodiversity, food security, and local livelihoods. The project is fully funded through the Dutch Research Agenda and the Brazilian research council CNPq . Within the project, you will closely collaborate with another PhD candidate at Utrecht University on spatial optimization of land use, as well as with several consortium members in Brazil, and the International Institute of Social Studies (ISS) at the Erasmus University Rotterdam.

REQUIREMENTS:
We are looking for candidates with:
 An MSc degree in (or nearing completion of) an MSc program in Human Geography, International Development Studies, Critical Agrarian Studies, or a related Social Science discipline;
 An interest in the impacts of land use on the environment and the livelihoods of people;
 Experience in qualitative field research and working with diverse groups of people and stakeholders;
 Proficiency in Portuguese;
 Proficiency in English; and
 The ability to work as part of an interdisciplinary research team.
The following qualifications are desirable but not required:
 Experience with participatory GIS and mapping techniques; or
 Experience with working with rural communities, Indigenous peoples and/or Quilombola.

academictransfer:363895.description:
Voor het onderwijsondersteuning-team van het departement Psychologie zoeken we een sociale en leergierige medewerker studentenzaken. In deze veelzijdige functie zorg je ervoor dat onderwijsactiviteiten achter de schermen soepel verlopen. Je ondersteunt docenten en studenten, houdt overzicht over uiteenlopende processen en zorgt dat de administratieve werkzaamheden zorgvuldig worden uitgevoerd. Heb jij plezier in organiseren, werk je graag nauwkeurig en pak je nieuwe informatie snel op? Dan maken we graag kennis met jou!
 Wat ga je doen? 
Samen met negen collega’s ondersteun je de bachelor- en masteropleidingen van het departement Psychologie. Je zorgt ervoor dat onderwijsprocessen goed georganiseerd zijn en zoekt daarbij steeds de juiste weg tussen de wensen van docenten en de opleiding, de mogelijkheden binnen het team en de kaders van de faculteit en universiteit. Als medewerker studentzaken heb je een afwisselend takenpakket. Je krijgt de ruimte om je te verdiepen in specifieke werkzaamheden en mee te denken over verbeteringen in processen en dienstverlening.
Samen met je collega’s:
 behandel je mails van docenten, studenten en studiekiezers;
 stel je roosters op in overleg met docenten en het facultaire roosterbureau;
 ondersteun je bij de organisatie van toetsen, inzagemomenten en cursusevaluaties;
 coördineer je de cursusinschrijving en werkgroepindeling;
 informeer je studenten over onderwijszaken;
 help je bij de organisatie van open dagen, diploma-uitreikingen, de selectie en de INtroductie;
 verzorg je de afgifte van certificaten en registraties zoals de vLOGO;
 houd je de jaarplanning van de verschillende processen in het oog en signaleer je tijdig knelpunten.
Sommige processen kunnen complex zijn en komen maar één keer per jaar voor. Het kan daarom even duren voordat je bent ingewerkt. Wij verwachten daarom niet dat je alles direct weet: nieuwsgierigheid, leergierigheid en de bereidheid om vragen te stellen vinden wij belangrijker.
Het Onderwijsondersteuningsteam Psychologie bestaat uit een aantal vaste ondersteuners waaronder twee participatiewet medewerkers en daarnaast wisselende studentassistenten. De teamleden zijn sociale en betrokkenen medewerkers en hoewel iedereen een duidelijke eigen verantwoordelijkheid heeft staan de medewerkers elkaar bij wanneer nodig. Er is in het team voldoende ruimte voor flexibiliteit en er wordt hybride gewerkt.

REQUIREMENTS:
Deze functie past perfect bij jou als je talent hebt voor het behouden van overzicht in een dynamische omgeving, leergierig bent en nauwkeurig werkt. Het snel eigen maken van nieuwe informatie, zoals nieuwe systemen of processen, is voor jou geen probleem. Je kunt zelfstandig werken, maar zoekt ook de samenwerking met andere teamleden op. Ook in drukke tijden houd je het hoofd koel, doe je de juiste dingen, help je anderen en wordt je humor gewaardeerd. Je hebt een groot verantwoordelijkheidsgevoel, zelfvertrouwen en behoefte om mee te denken en te helpen. Je ziet waar processen efficiënter kunnen en bewaakt de tijdslijn van taken.
En verder:
 je werkt gestructureerd en zorgt voor een zorgvuldige administratie;
 je schakelt makkelijk met collega's en zoekt actief de samenwerking op;
 je haalt plezier uit het zoeken naar creatieve oplossingen en neemt initiatief;
 je drukt je makkelijk uit (schriftelijk en mondeling) in het Nederlands en Engels;
 ervaring met Microsoft Office is vanzelfsprekend, kennis van Osiris is een grote pré. Uitgebreide kennis van Excel, of de motivatie om je dit snel eigen te maken, is heel wenselijk.

academictransfer:363911.description:
Voor de afdeling Gezelschapsdieren bij het Universitair Dierenziekenhuis zoeken we een enthousiaste en gedreven allround Paraveterinair voor de spoedkliniek Midden Nederland. Je komt op een super interessante en dynamische plek terecht!
 Wat ga je doen? 
Bij de Spoedkliniek Gezelschapsdieren Midden Nederland, onderdeel van het Universitair Dierenziekenhuis, werk je in een dynamische omgeving samen met internationale topspecialisten, bevlogen dierenartsen en ervaren paraveterinairen. In ons team staat samenwerking centraal en delen collega’s graag hun kennis en ervaring met elkaar. Als Paraveterinair maak je deel uit van een hecht team dat 24 uur per dag, 7 dagen per week klaarstaat voor onze patiënten. Geen dienst is hetzelfde: samen met je collega’s verzorg je de baliewerkzaamheden en zorg je voor de dierverpleegkundige zorg van dieren in de opname. We hebben een divers patiënten aanbod je kunt hier echt hét verschil maken! Werken in een universitair dierenziekenhuis betekent ook dat je een rol speelt in het opleiden van de volgende generatie dierenartsen. Je begeleidt studenten Diergeneeskunde die tijdens hun klinische stages praktijkervaring opdoen in onze kliniek. Bij ons krijg je als paraveterinair veel verantwoordelijkheid en ruimte om je vaardigheden verder te ontwikkelen. Je werkt met complexe spoedgevallen, leert continu bij van specialisten en collega’s, en groeit elke dag verder in je vak.

REQUIREMENTS:
Je hebt met succes je opleiding ‘paraveterinair’ afgerond en hebt enkele jaren ervaring in 1ste, 2de of 3de lijn kliniek;
 je kunt goed plannen en organiseren, ben je in grote mate in staat zelfstandig patiënten te verplegen en los je onverwachte problemen samen op met je collega’s;
 je bent communicatief sterk, stressbestendig en flexibel;
 je werkt graag in een afwisselend 24-uurs rooster met dag-, avond- en nachtdiensten en draait ook regelmatig weekenddiensten.
 Goede beheersing van de Nederlandse taal goed, zowel mondeling als schriftelijk.
 Goede beheersing van de Engelse taal;
 je woont binnen een reisafstand van maximaal 45 minuten van Utrecht i.v.m. onregelmatige werktijden.
Twijfel je of je helemaal aan het profiel voldoet? Reageer gerust we kijken graag samen of er een match is.
